---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Person",
  "name": "Kseniia Shermin",
  "jobTitle": "Performance Marketing Manager",
  "description": "10+ years of experience in paid search and paid social, specializing in lead generation for B2B and service businesses.",
  "url": "https://kseniiashermin.com/about"
};
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="Performance Marketing Manager with 10+ years of experience in paid search and paid social lead generation."
  schema={schema}
>
  <section>
    <h1>About Me</h1>
    <p>
      I'm a Performance Marketing Manager with 10+ years of experience in paid search
      and paid social. I manage large-scale Google Ads and Microsoft Ads accounts &mdash;
      spending over $100,000 per day &mdash; alongside Meta Ads, LinkedIn Ads, and other
      PPC platforms, working with any bidding logic or campaign structure the account
      requires. My focus is lead generation: turning "we just need more leads" into a
      predictable, forecastable pipeline.
    </p>

    <!-- TODO: add Google Ads / Microsoft Advertising certifications here as trust badges -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How I Work</h2>
    <div class="split-cards">
      <div class="split-card">
        <h3>Start with the goal, not the tactic</h3>
        <p>I focus on the number that actually matters &mdash; usually lowering cost per action and scaling conversion volume at the same time, not one at the expense of the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-card">
        <h3>Fix the foundation first</h3>
        <p>Before touching bids or creative, I make sure tracking and data are accurate. Every scaling decision after that is only as good as the data it's built on.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-card">
        <h3>Then experiment relentlessly</h3>
        <p>Once the foundation is solid, I test across every layer of the account &mdash; bidding strategy, audience signals, ad copy, landing pages &mdash; to find what actually moves the number.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What Clients &amp; Colleagues Say</h2>
    <!-- TODO: replace with real LinkedIn recommendations / Upwork reviews once collected -->
    <p class="updated-note">Reviews coming soon.</p>
  </section>
</Layout>
